Title:
Lockjaw – Advanced Windows C2 Framework (v0.2.15)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Lockjaw is an alpha-stage, modular Windows command-and-control framework with a Rust (Tokio/Axum) teamserver, a Zig-based implant, and PIC assembly/PowerShell stagers. It focuses on stealthy post-exploitation by combining indirect syscalls (Hell’s Gate/Halo’s Gate), IAT-less API resolution via PEB walking, and an AMSI bypass implemented with hardware breakpoints and vectored exception handling. The implant supports process migration via reflective manual mapping using section mapping (NtCreateSection/NtMapViewOfSection) and a PoolParty-style threadpool worker factory technique to avoid remote thread creation telemetry. It includes an in-memory COFF/BOF execution engine with partial Beacon API compatibility, plus HTTP(S) and DNS (TXT) transports with RC4-encrypted tasking/results. Operators interact through a Python async TUI client and a REST API; the teamserver can dynamically cross-compile payloads using Zig. Useful primarily for red teams and adversary emulation, and interesting for its combination of modern language split (Rust/Zig) with multiple evasion and injection primitives.

Technical Focus:
- Indirect syscalls (Hell’s Gate / Halo’s Gate) and SSN recovery from in-memory ntdll
- AMSI bypass via HWBP (DR0/DR7) + Vectored Exception Handling (AddVectoredExceptionHandler)
- IAT-less dynamic API resolution (PEB traversal, DJB2 hashing)
- Reflective manual mapping via section objects (NtCreateSection/NtMapViewOfSection) and NtCreateThreadEx
- PoolParty / threadpool worker factory injection (TpWorkerFactory, NtSetInformationWorkerFactory)
- DNS tunneling over TXT records (Base32 + RC4) and HTTP(S) WinHTTP transport

Use Cases:
- Build and operate a custom Windows C2 for red team engagements/adversary simulation
- Test EDR visibility against indirect syscalls, IAT-clean implants, and HWBP/VEH AMSI bypass
- Perform stealthy process migration without classic remote thread creation signals (PoolParty)
- Execute BOFs/COFF objects in-memory for post-exploitation tradecraft
- Use DNS-based C2 in restricted egress environments; rotate listeners dynamically at runtime

Keywords:
Lockjaw, C2 framework, Rust, Tokio, Axum, Rustls, Zig, Windows implant, PIC stager, WinHTTP, DNS tunneling, TXT records, Base32, RC4, indirect syscalls, Hell’s Gate, Halo’s Gate, ntdll, SSN, PEB traversal, IAT-less, DJB2 hashing, AMSI, AmsiScanBuffer, hardware breakpoints, DR0 DR7, VEH, AddVectoredExceptionHandler, reflective loading, manual mapping, NtCreateSection, NtMapViewOfSection, NtCreateThreadEx, PoolParty, TpWorkerFactory, NtQuerySystemInformation, COFF loader, BOF, SQLite, SQLx